Allocated id namespace. One empty file per id, created by
`sh spikes/harness/allocid.sh <PREFIX>` with `set -o noclobber`, so the
test-and-create is one O_EXCL open and twenty racing lanes get twenty distinct
ids. See H45.

Tracked deliberately: an id allocated by a lane that then crashes before
publishing must stay taken. That unpublished window is exactly where the two
real collisions of 2026-08-17 landed (H30: AGENT-1 vs ATTACKER-1; H38: ok-1 vs
ATTACKER-1) -- both lanes ran a correct grep about two minutes apart and neither
row existed yet at the other's read.

THERE IS NO RELEASE PATH, and that is deliberate: freeing an id re-opens the
race it was allocated to close, and ids are free while a duplicate citation is
not (73 of them across 12 files under H18).

H46 and H47 were consumed by the allocator's own demonstration -- allocate twice,
show the ids differ -- and are recorded here rather than quietly reused. A gap
with a reason is cheaper than a reuse without one.

`.seeded.<PREFIX>` marks that the one-time bootstrap grep has run for that
prefix. After it, no grep is on the allocation path.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESERVED FIXTURES — DO NOT DELETE THE FILES THAT HAVE NO QUEUE ROW.
(H64, ATOM-3, 2026-08-17. Everything above this line is ok-1's, 853a77c.)

    H91  H99  S96  S97  S98  S99

These six are RESERVED FIXTURES, not allocations. They are the synthetic ids
inside the selfcheck strings of `journalcheck.py`, `idscope.py` and
`refcheck.py`, chosen high so a real allocation would not reach them. Nothing
enforced that: `allocid.sh` deliberately does not scan code (H57's attack
measured why -- scanning code reserves `Q2`, `Q3`, `B6`, `B16`, which are
variable names and grep flags), so the allocator cannot see a fixture. When real
allocation reaches H91, `refcheck` check 5 and `idscope` read a selfcheck string
as a queue row.

They are reserved today only by ACCIDENT: `WORK_QUEUE.md`'s H64 row names them
in prose, the seed reads every tracked `*.md`, so the row documenting the hazard
reserved the ids it warns about. Measured 2026-08-17: the highest real `CLAIM`
was H68 while `.ids/H91` and `.ids/H99` already existed.

An accident is not a mechanism. These six are the only ids in the pool with no
queue row and no claim, which makes them exactly what a tidy-up would delete
first, and deleting one silently restores the collision with no error anywhere.
`spikes/harness/test_h64_id_reservations.sh` fails if one goes missing or
becomes untracked. To retire a reservation: delete the fixture id from the
module that uses it FIRST, then remove the file. Never the other way round.

The id files MUST STAY EMPTY. `allocid.sh:118` re-seeds from the tree on every
invocation with `: > "$IDS/$i"`, which truncates -- anything written into an id
file is erased by the next allocation. That is why this warning is in a README
beside them and not inside them.

CORRECTION to the line above about `.seeded.<PREFIX>`: it is stale. H57 v2
DELETED that guard -- the seed now runs on every invocation, because the guard
"froze the free list". The sentence is left in place above rather than edited,
since the file is ok-1's; this is the changelog line §5 asks for.
